  "abstract": "Mou dao yin jie is a one-volume late Qing Chinese Christian tract by Xiu Huayang (Francis Huberty James). The title leaf of this woodblock edition is dated 1889 (Guangxu 15) and bears the imprint of Tongwenzhai in Jinan Prefecture, Shandong. The dialogue opens by asking who gives people life and sustains them. It cites the Book of Odes and the Book of Documents and explains the relationship between Heaven in the Confucian classics and God (Shangdi). The questions then turn to how Heaven and human beings respond to one another, how conscience distinguishes good from evil, and how people can repent, do good, and pray. The closing Morning and Evening Prayer for Seekers of the Way thanks God in the first person for giving and sustaining life and for teaching, then asks for help in amending one’s conduct and preparing for the life to come. The dialogue and prayer offer material for studying how late Qing missionaries used the Confucian classics and their moral vocabulary to explain Christian belief and daily practice.\n\nThis dataset uses 26 fixed images from Harvard-Yenching Library DRS 428685382. The main text is transcribed from 15 pages. The AI-reviewed JSON, TXT and TEI transcription retains 31 unresolved comparison items. Full human collation is outstanding.",
